@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# Waku mirror notifications
+
+This optional adapter observes bot-submitted jobs after Mirrorz's existing authorization
+and task checks allow them. It reports lifecycle events; it does not replace the
+mirror command, downloader, uploader, permission filters or task ownership.
+
+Waku also works with unmodified Mirrorz by observing its native replies and status
+messages. Install this adapter only for exact structured receipts, more reliable
+result correlation and persisted event replay. Until a structured receipt arrives,
+Waku uses Mirrorz's normal status command; afterward it can use `/wakustatus`.
+
+## Setup
+
+1. Add Waku and Mirrorz to the mirror group and enable Telegram bot communication
+   so they can receive one another's messages.
+2. Give Waku permission to submit tasks using Mirrorz's existing authorization
+   settings. There is no new bridge ID setting and no automatic privilege grant.
+3. Configure the mirror group ID and Mirrorz username on Waku, which limits mirror
+   requests to its bot administrators.
+4. If installing this optional adapter, rebuild/recreate Mirrorz with these changes.
+   Deploy the updated Waku source independently.
+
+Normal Mirrorz checks remain active, including token verification, subscription,
+BOT_PM, rate/task/size limits and queue rules. If a check prevents a job, Mirrorz
+sends its usual response and Waku observes that reply. Configure these existing
+checks appropriately for the bot account; the adapter does not bypass them.
+
+The supplied Compose file mounts the named volume `mirror_bridge_data` at
+`/usr/src/app/bridge_data`. Keep this volume across container recreation. Other
+Docker setups should mount persistent storage at the same path. Do not put this
+database in `downloads`, which Mirrorz cleans at startup.
+
+## Protocol
+
+Waku sends a normal mirror command with a correlation marker on the second line:
+
+```text
+/mirror@MirrorBot https://example.org/file.zip
+Waku-Request: 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
+```
+
+Only an actual bot sender in a group with a valid two-line marker receives lifecycle
+notifications. This marker grants no permissions. Bookkeeping starts lazily when a
+normal task reaches a lifecycle callback. Mirrorz replies to the original command,
+mentions the requesting bot and emits JSON after `WAKU_MIRROR_EVENT `.
+
+Required fields are `v: 1`, `request_id`, `event` and `command_message_id`. Events
+are `accepted` (download or queue task exists), `completed`, `failed` and `cancelled`.
+Optional fields include `gid`, `name`, `size`, `cancel_command`, `links` (label/url)
+and `error`. An internal `received` record may exist while the first event is being
+persisted. Partial upload success retains actual provider errors; all providers
+failing is reported as a failure.
+
+The most recent event is persisted before delivery. The requesting bot can recover
+it using `/wakustatus@MirrorBot <request_id>` from the same group; the stored sender
+ID and group ID must match. Other callers receive no data. An interrupted old task
+missing after a restart is reported as failed. Use the returned `cancel_command`,
+normally `/c_<gid>@MirrorBot`, with Mirrorz's normal authorization and task-owner rules.
+
+Waku prevents duplicate outbound requests. This adapter does not intercept command
+execution or deduplicate downloads inside Mirrorz. Human-facing responses are
+composed by Waku's AI. Native replies cover failures before lifecycle bookkeeping.
